The conversation focused on refining a paragraph in James's report concerning the relationship between welfare, climate impacts, and GDP. The initial exploration highlighted that climate-related welfare losses exceed reductions in economic production, specifically pointing out health risks to vulnerable populations. A reviewer suggested that the last sentence could be clearer and seemed to conflate welfare with GDP. In response, I crafted a clearer revision emphasizing that while welfare damages are expressed in "percent-of-GDP" terms for comparability, this does not equate to actual GDP losses. The revision underscored the importance of understanding non-market impacts on society alongside economic indicators.
